\description{
\code{get_traits()} is the main entry point for pulling AVONET trait data into R.
It accepts taxon names at any rank, resolves each one to the species it
contains, queries the ecological, mass and geographical trait tables in a
single batched call, and returns the data together with the metadata and
literature sources needed to cite it.
}
\details{
Names supplied to \code{species} are resolved one at a time, so a single call can
mix ranks, e.g. \code{c("Buteo", "Falco peregrinus", "Accipitridae")}. Each name
is auto-detected as a species binomial, genus, family or order unless \code{rank}
forces one interpretation for all of them. Resolved species that turn out to
have no rows in the trait tables trigger a warning naming the species and
the tables they are missing from, rather than disappearing silently.
\subsection{Filtering}{

\code{filter} is a named list applied after the query returns. Element names
accept either the full SQL column name or the short name left once the table
prefix is stripped (\code{habitat} for \code{ect_habitat}, \code{range_size} for
\code{spd_range_size}, \code{mass} for \code{mass_value}). Character columns take a single
value or a set of values; numeric columns take a \code{list(op = , val = )} pair,
where \code{op} is one of \code{"=="}, \code{"!="}, \code{"<"}, \code{"<="}, \code{">"} or \code{">="}.
Multiple conditions are combined with AND.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{filter = list(habitat = "Forest")
filter = list(trophic_niche = c("Frugivore", "Nectarivore"))
filter = list(range_size = list(op = "<", val = 1000))
filter = list(habitat = "Forest", min_latitude = list(op = ">", val = 40))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\subsection{Resolution}{

The ecological, mass and geographical traits are species-level: one row per
species. Morphological measurements are specimen-level: many rows per
species. Rather than joining the two and repeating every species-level value
across each specimen, \code{resolution = "specimen"} returns the measurements as
a separate \code{specimen_data} element, so \code{data} stays one row per species
either way. Specimens are fetched only for the species that survive
\code{filter}, and species with no specimen records trigger a warning.
